# paramfunc
Decorator and class for easy argument conversion and validation..

Github: [link](https://github.com/Lightseeker7/paramfunc)

Project: [link](https://pypi.org/project/paramfunc/)

# @parameterize
The function decorator takes up to two arguments, `params` and `kwparams`, and converts the function into a callable `ParamFunc` object. This `ParamFunc` object can be called as a normal function can, only it will first convert the arguments and check them against any related parameters.

Formatted as follows:
```
@parameterize(params=<params>,kwparams=<kwparams>)
def my_func(arg1,arg2):
    return arg1 + arg2
```
In the event a converted argument does not satisfy a parameter, it will throw an error. Any errors involved with converting the argument will also occur, where applicable.
## params
Set to `None` if none are given.

`params` is used for all positional-only arguments, what would be called with `*args`

The format for `params` is a `dict` object formatted like the following:
```
{"convert":convert,"params":[boolean functions]}
```
An example of a `params` argument that converts all input items in `*args` to `int` objects and checks if they are even and not negative:
```
{"convert":int,"params":[lambda x:x>=0,lambda x:x%2==0]}
```
## kwparams
Set to `None` if none are given.

`kwparams` is used for all other arguments, whether they are keyword-only or positional with a keyword.

The format for `kwparams` is a `dict` object formatted like the following:
```
{"keyword1":{"convert":convert,"params":[boolean functions]},"keyword2":{"convert":convert,"params":[boolean functions]}}
```
An example of a `kwparams` argument that checks if the `"file"` argument is a .txt file and the `"contents"` is a list of strings:
```
{"file":{"convert":str,"params":[lambda x:x[-4:]==".txt",lambda x:len(x)>=5]},"contents":{"convert":list,"params":[lambda x:all(isinstance(item,str) for item in x)]}}
```
If a keyword argument has no need for conversion or for checking against a variable, it can be omitted from `kwparams`.
## "convert"
`"convert"` can be a `type` object such as `int` or `str`, or it can be any function that takes the related argument and returns a value. This can be a `lambda` or a function defined elsewhere in the program. If no change should happen to the related argument(s), but there still needs to be `"params"` for it, it should be set to the following:
```
"convert":lambda x:x
```
## "params"
`"params"` is a list of functions that all must return boolean values. These can be `lambda` functions or functions defined elsewhere in the program. If no parameters are needed for the argument, but it needs to be converted with `"convert"`, it should just be set to an empty list:
```
"params":[]
```
# ParamFunc
`@paramterize` converts the function it is decorating into a `ParamFunc` object. This object has three instance variables:

`func`: the function that was decorated

`params`: the input `params` argument in the decoration, `None` if none was given.

`kwparams`: the input `kwparams` argument in the decoration, `None` if none was given. 

The `ParamFunc` object can be called as a function. It will take the arguments given it, convert them using the related `"convert"` functions, and check them against any related `"params"` functions with `assert param(converted_argument)`.
